@@ -51,3 +51,21 @@ The document, patch, and each `value` member must be JSON values. In particular,
 sparse arrays, accessors, and non-JSON object types are rejected. Operations run in array order and
 stop at the first error; the supplied document remains unchanged. Additional operation members are
 ignored as specified by RFC 6902.
+
+## JSON Merge Patch
+
+`applyMergePatch(target: unknown, patch: unknown): unknown`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies
+an [RFC 7386 JSON Merge Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386) and returns a new JSON value.
+Object patches merge recursively; a `null` member removes that member from the target, while a
+non-object patch replaces the entire target.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from './src/index.js';
+
+const target = { title: 'Draft', author: { name: 'Ada', active: true } };
+const updated = applyMergePatch(target, { title: 'Published', author: { active: null } });
+// target is unchanged; updated is { title: 'Published', author: { name: 'Ada' } }.
+```
+
+The target and patch must be JSON values. The function does not modify either input, and the result
+does not retain mutable references to values from either input.
